WINNER: CURVCELL-SID
FIRST LISTED: cocond-markov-poincare-ot
ARGUMENT: (1) behavior_evidence and downstream_mechanism: the bundle's strongest measured constraint is a capacity-allocation failure, not a sharpness-of-argmin failure. E8 gives the share of canonical held-out pairs that share one level-1 code as 0.0822 at K=256 versus 0.3106 at K=16 (negatives 0.0129 vs 0.1352), and E6 shows the best variants still lose the gold prefix at depths 2-3 (alive 1.000/0.829/0.161/0.085 for l1_br_euclid), i.e. the per-level budget is the bottleneck. B acts on exactly that budget: per-code s[l][k] is derived from measured branching, the same train-only transition counts behind E5 (H from 0.931 to 3.405, top-1 mass 0.4375 to 0.0543) and E10 (mean_outgoing_degree 5.4526, edge density 5.33), and its observables (Spearman |A_k| vs B_k; H(next code | level-0 code)) are auditable from those counts. A's behaviour enters through a batch-restricted transition operator B_bt in a 1024-item minibatch; with E10's degree of ~5.45, that submatrix is near-identity most steps, which A's own risk list concedes degrades it to plain curved Sinkhorn, and it leaves 256 equal-sized cells untouched. (2) geometry_necessity: A's curvature is fixed kappa = -1, and that exact family is already a measured loser to its own Euclidean control in the ledger: E6 l1_br recall@10 0.04671 versus l1_br_euclid 0.06207, and successor_geodesic_coarse 0.05500 versus l1_only 0.06233. A's genuinely new content is the OT plan, not the geometry, so the curvature is inherited and unargued (rubric level 3 at best), whereas B's field is behaviour-derived, its behaviour-off control collapses the field to a single unit curvature, and the encoder-cancellation path is named as a diagnostic rather than hoped away (level 4). A does score higher on novelty - none of the listed rounds diffused the assignment plan - but novelty is 20 percent and it rides on a 20 percent dimension where E6 contradicts it. (3) implementability and falsifiability: B is +1.05M FLOPs, 12 refreshes at under 2 s each, no extra parameters, detached and clamped s, and a single discriminating falsifier (Spearman near zero means the encoder is absorbing the behaviour); A needs 3 Sinkhorn sweeps plus a sparse (B x B) @ (B x K) product per level every step, a median-frozen tau, a usage prior q, straight-through gradients and a Klein-space barycenter that can drift to the ball boundary, and its null is confounded between too-sparse transitions (E10) and the wrong cost kernel, so it buys less information per unit of the frozen budget.
FAILURE RISK: The encoder cancels B's rescaling: dividing d_B by s[l][k] is only a per-code monotone rescaling of distance, so the argmin may barely move and the fixed-point loop can drift toward a few huge low-curvature cells or oscillate at the refresh epochs. If Spearman(|A_k|, B_k) stays near zero and Recall@10 stays at the measured ceiling, the run cannot separate "encoder absorbs the curvature" from "prefix coherence does not convert to recall" - the exact negative that E6 already reports for this family. If that per-code divisor is judged Euclidean-reproducible, B's geometry_necessity falls to rubric level 2 and the comparison flips to A on novelty and exploration value.